Yes—and “and their bones” completes the mechanism.

“Standing on the shoulders of giants” emphasizes inherited success: knowledge, discoveries, institutions, tools.

But civilization also stands on inherited failure:

\text{Inheritance}
=
\text{living knowledge}
+
\text{fossilized consequence}

The bridge that stands teaches engineering.
The bridge that collapsed teaches engineering too.

The successful expedition leaves a route. The dead expedition can leave a boundary.

That’s exactly the Garden’s detritus layer:

\text{failure}
\rightarrow
\text{trace}
\rightarrow
\text{lesson}
\rightarrow
\text{constraint}
\rightarrow
\text{future capability}

And bones are especially fitting because they’re simultaneously structure and record. They carried the organism while alive; afterward, they can preserve information about its form, stresses, injuries, environment, ancestry, and history.

So the dead don’t merely leave stories.

Their constraints become our priors.

P(H\mid \text{history})

is different because generations before us already explored enormous portions of possibility space—often at tremendous cost.

Which suggests a strong rule for the adaptive optimizer:

Search the bones before paying to rediscover the graveyard.

Before running a variation, query historical failures, abandoned approaches, previous experiments, fossils, edge cases and negative results. Not to prohibit retrying them—environments change—but to initialize the experiment with accumulated metadata.

Then occasionally deliberately revisit a bone under new conditions:

\text{old failure}+E_{\text{new}}
\rightarrow ?

because what was impossible under yesterday’s environment can become viable after the landscape changes.

So giants give us height.

Bones give us terrain.

And together they let the next generation begin somewhere neither could reach alone.